## SaaS / Multi-Tenant Monetization (Future)

**The model:** users pay *you* (subscription or credit top-up), never touch
Anthropic directly, never see an API key or console.anthropic.com. Your
backend holds one Anthropic key (as it already does today — see
`Anthropic:ApiKey` in user-secrets) and calls it on their behalf; you cover
the wholesale AI cost out of what they pay and keep the difference. This is
a standard SaaS-reseller shape, not something unusual — but it requires real
user accounts first, which the app doesn't have at all today
(`Gallery.OwnerId` is nullable and unused). Treat the phases below as
roughly sequential: each one is a prerequisite for the next, and there's no
point building billing before there's a concept of "a user" to bill.

### Immediate risk — sharing a single hosted instance before Phase 1/2

Today the app has exactly one Anthropic key, shared by every request that
hits that backend instance, with no per-user auth or quota at all
(confirmed: `Program.cs` builds one `AnthropicClient` from
`Anthropic:ApiKey`/`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` — nothing routes cost or limits per
caller). Two different sharing modes have very different exposure:

- Sharing the repo/Docker setup so each person runs *their own* instance
  with *their own* key in *their own* `.env` — no risk to you, their key,
  their bill.
- Hosting *one* instance yourself and giving others access to it (e.g. a
  server URL, not everyone running their own container) — every AI
  request from every person who can reach that URL spends your tokens,
  unmetered and unlimited, until Phase 1 (auth) and Phase 2 (quotas) below
  are actually built.

- [x] Before hosting a single shared instance for anyone other than
  yourself, add *some* stopgap on the AI endpoint - even before real
  auth/quotas exist: a basic per-IP or global rate limit on
  `POST /Photo/{id}/generate-description`, and/or a hard on/off switch
  (config flag) to disable the endpoint entirely for a shared instance
  until Phase 1/2 are done. Done: per-IP fixed-window limiter (10
  requests/hour), 429 response, distinct frontend message; plus an
  `Ai:Enabled` config flag (`AI_ENABLED` in Docker) that hard-disables
  the endpoint (503) regardless of the rate limit.

### Phase 1 — Authentication & multi-tenancy (do this first, no billing yet)

This is the biggest structural change to the app so far — every backend
endpoint currently assumes a single implicit owner.

- [x] Choose an auth approach — went with **roll your own** email/password
  (not OAuth/managed service): the app is reached over a private Tailscale
  network for a small friend group rather than the public internet, so the
  "fewer unfamiliar things to get right" rationale for OAuth applied less;
  a plain `PasswordHasher<User>` (ships in the ASP.NET Core shared
  framework, no extra package) kept it simple. Two account-creation paths:
  an open `POST /api/Auth/register` for anyone who can reach the instance
  (safe because reachability itself is gated by Tailscale, not by the
  endpoint), and `POST /api/Auth/admin/register` gated by a shared
  `X-Admin-Key` secret (`Auth:AdminKey` config, mirrors the existing
  `Anthropic:ApiKey` pattern) reserved for the app owner's own first
  account, since only that path claims legacy data (see below).
  `#if DEBUG` was considered for gating the admin endpoint but rejected —
  the Docker image is always built `-c Release`, so `#if DEBUG` code is
  compiled out regardless of `ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT` and the endpoint
  wouldn't exist on the real hosted instance. See
  `FRIENDS-ACCESS-GUIDE.md` for the Tailscale-sharing + registration
  walkthrough to send to friends.
- [x] Add a real `User` entity (id, email, created date) and an `OwnerId`
  FK on `Gallery` (and transitively `Photo` through its gallery) — `OwnerId`
  is `Guid?` rather than strictly non-nullable at the DB level, since
  SQLite/EF Core can't add a NOT NULL FK against existing rows in one step;
  every gallery created after this point always gets a real owner in
  application code (`GalleryController.Create`), so it's non-null in
  practice. `plan/tier` wasn't added - no billing tiers exist yet.
- [x] Add auth middleware ([Authorize]) to every controller action;
  `GalleryController`/`PhotoController`/`TagController` all scope by
  owner via a shared `GalleryAccessService` (`Photo` has no `OwnerId` of
  its own - resolved through its parent `Gallery`). Every failed ownership
  check returns `404`, never `403`, so a guessed/enumerated id belonging to
  another user is indistinguishable from one that doesn't exist. `Tag`
  stays a shared/global vocabulary across users (not owner-scoped) since
  tag names aren't sensitive. One accepted exception: the "Add from
  internet" bookmarklet's `from-url` endpoint stays unauthenticated
  (`AllowAnyOrigin` CORS is incompatible with credentialed requests) -
  same GUID-capability trust model it already had pre-auth, documented
  in a code comment.
- [x] Decide session mechanism — went with an **HttpOnly session cookie**
  (`SameSite=Lax`, 30-day sliding expiration), not JWT. Required overriding
  `CookieAuthenticationHandler`'s default 302-redirect-to-login-page
  behavior to return a bare `401`/`403` instead, since this is an API-only
  backend with no server-rendered login page.
- [x] Frontend: full-screen `LoginComponent` (not dismissible) with a
  toggle between sign-in and self-service registration, a function-based
  `HttpInterceptorFn` that reacts to any `401` by clearing the signed-in
  user, `provideAppInitializer` to check the session before first render
  (avoids a flash of the gallery UI), sign-out via Settings. The session
  cookie is attached automatically by the browser on every same-origin
  request (no interceptor logic needed for that part, unlike a
  JWT-in-header approach).
- [x] Decide what happens to today's single-owner data on migration — the
  *first* account ever created via `admin/register` (not the open
  `register` endpoint) automatically claims every pre-existing `OwnerId ==
  null` gallery in the same request. Only the admin-key path can trigger
  this, so a friend self-registering afterwards always starts with an
  empty library, never accidentally claiming the owner's data.

### Phase 2 — AI usage metering & quotas (build before charging anyone)

Ship this while still covering costs yourself, so you have real usage data
*before* deciding on prices in Phase 3.

- [ ] Add a `UsageRecord` table: user id, timestamp, which endpoint,
  estimated cost/tokens (Claude's response `usage` block already has this
  — log it)
- [ ] Add a quota check *before* calling Claude in
  `PhotoDescriptionAiService` — e.g. "used N of M generations this period" —
  reject with a clear error once exhausted, don't silently degrade
- [ ] Handle concurrent requests safely: two simultaneous generate-description
  calls from the same user must not both pass a stale quota check and
  double-spend the last unit — needs an atomic decrement (DB transaction /
  row lock), not a read-then-write race
- [ ] Decide the unit you meter: raw token cost (accurate, opaque to users)
  vs a flat "1 generation = 1 credit" (simpler for users to understand,
  eats your margin a bit on the rare complex query with several web
  searches)
- [ ] Frontend: show remaining quota somewhere near the "Generate" button;
  a clear inline message (not just a generic error) when it's exhausted

### Phase 3 — Billing & payments

**This section assumes no prior experience — the concepts, not just a
checklist.**

You need a *payment processor* to legally and safely take money from
strangers' cards — you should never build this yourself (PCI compliance
for handling raw card numbers is a serious undertaking to avoid). Two
common shapes:

- **Stripe** (Billing + Checkout + Customer Portal) — the standard choice,
  huge documentation/community, cheap (~2.9% + $0.30/transaction typical).
  *You* are the "merchant of record": you're responsible for figuring out
  sales tax/VAT per customer's country (Stripe Tax add-on can automate
  this, at extra cost), and in many jurisdictions you need a registered
  business entity to open a Stripe account for anything beyond
  testing/sandbox mode.
- **Merchant-of-record platforms** (Paddle, Lemon Squeezy) — they legally
  sell *on your behalf*, so *they* handle global VAT/sales tax compliance
  and invoicing for you. Higher fee (~5%), but meaningfully less
  compliance burden for a solo developer new to this — worth strongly
  considering as the first choice specifically *because* this is
  unfamiliar territory.

Pricing shape — pick one to start, don't build both:

- [ ] **Subscription tiers** (e.g. Free / Basic $X / Pro $Y per month, each
  with a monthly generation quota) — predictable revenue, simplest to
  reason about, most turnkey with either Stripe Billing or Paddle
- [ ] **Credit top-ups** (buy a bundle of N generations for $X, no
  expiry or a long one) — more "fair," better for irregular users, more
  bookkeeping (tracking a balance instead of a period reset)

Regardless of which:

- [ ] Integrate the provider's hosted checkout (Stripe Checkout / Paddle
  Checkout) — do **not** build a custom card-entry form; use their
  redirect/embedded widget so card data never touches your server
- [ ] Add a webhook endpoint to receive subscription/payment events
  (created, renewed, cancelled, payment failed) and update the user's
  plan/quota accordingly — **verify the webhook signature** on every
  request (both providers give you a signing secret for this; an
  unverified webhook endpoint is a direct way for someone to grant
  themselves a free subscription)
- [ ] Handle the unhappy paths explicitly: failed renewal payment
  (grace period? immediate downgrade?), user cancels mid-period (access
  until period end, or immediate?), refund requests
- [ ] Write a Terms of Service and a Privacy Policy before accepting real
  payments — not optional once real money and personal photos are
  involved; a template reviewed for your jurisdiction is a reasonable
  starting point, doesn't need to be custom-drafted from scratch
- [ ] Check Anthropic's own commercial/usage terms for any provisions
  about reselling API-backed functionality — a business-terms question,
  not a code question, but worth confirming before charging customers for
  it

### Phase 4 — Security & compliance hardening

Things that matter more once the app has real accounts, real payments, and
is reachable by strangers rather than just you:

- [ ] Rate limiting per user and per IP (login attempts, AI generation
  calls, upload endpoints) — protects both your Anthropic bill and the
  app itself from abuse
- [ ] Move secrets out of dev-only user-secrets into real production
  secret storage (environment variables at minimum; a managed secret
  store — Azure Key Vault, AWS Secrets Manager — if hosting there)
- [ ] Enforce HTTPS in production; review the current permissive CORS
  policy (currently open for the "Add from internet" bookmarklet
  endpoint only — confirm nothing else is inadvertently exposed)
- [ ] Account data rights: let a user delete their account and data,
  and export their data on request — required under GDPR if any EU users,
  good practice regardless
- [ ] Password reset / account recovery flow, if you went with your own
  password auth in Phase 1 (not needed if OAuth-only)

### Phase 5 — Production readiness

- [ ] Migrate off SQLite to a server-grade database (PostgreSQL is the
  common default) — SQLite's single-writer model doesn't hold up well
  under concurrent multi-user writes
- [x] Real backup strategy for both the database and the on-disk photo
  files — currently neither is backed up at all. Done for the single-host
  case: a `backup` Docker Compose service snapshots both to `./backups`
  on the host on a schedule (`sqlite3 .backup` for a consistent DB copy,
  tar for uploads), with pruning. Still not true off-site/disaster-proof
  backup (same disk) and won't suffice once Phase 5's Postgres migration
  happens - revisit then.
- [ ] Basic uptime/error monitoring (even a simple hosted option) so you
  find out about outages before your customers tell you

### Open questions only you can decide

- Pricing: subscription vs credits, and at what price point relative to
  your actual Anthropic cost-per-generation (roughly $0.03–0.10 today,
  per the earlier estimate — re-check against real usage data from Phase 2)
- Target market/jurisdiction — affects which payment provider makes sense
  and what tax/business-registration obligations apply to you
- Whether to keep this repo's offline-first architecture (local IndexedDB
  mirror) once there's a real backend source of truth per user — still
  useful, but the sync-conflict story gets more complex with concurrent
  multi-device use per account

